#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 840.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106562} :activity/id #uuid "a038488b-d4de-5f2b-9c61-0a4bc91a3757" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050394 :topic/id #uuid "455f5bc8-b430-5c8e-bb46-a11907e59c78" :topic/math-academy-id 1295} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106562 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107166 :tutorial/content "The **vector projection** of a vector $\\mathbf{a}$ onto another vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is the portion that the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends along $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFor example, consider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ shown below. Suppose we know that $|\\mathbf{a}|=2,$ $|\\mathbf{b}|=3,$ and the angle between the vectors is $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}.$\n\n![](images/a2/a28278cf12885ab7f845407ac2fa7f4ffa00dbe30849a3f8ff41f02e134ed3a0.png)\n\nThe starting point of the vector projection is $O,$ and the ending point will be the point $P$ that lies on the line containing $\\mathbf{b}$ with $AP \\perp \\mathbf{b},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f0b267fb009e79eb16f86be42edc2c835808b28aa3eaf1c7e857300224ee01ec.png)\n\nThe vector $\\overrightarrow{OP}$ is called the **vector projection** of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}.$ It is denoted as $\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a},$ and it is computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =OP\\cdot u \\\\  & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} = |\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,| \\cos\\theta$ is the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$ is a unit vector in the direction of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\nFirst, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5962a19e-bb12-54d8-b53a-24578711fa53" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9051 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "26031206-512a-5b41-90d5-299987889655" :step/math-academy-id 121755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106650}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105701 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105478 :question/id #uuid "3278333b-a29e-54c5-bc94-b5525bc6f751" :question/math-academy-id "e-14617"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c9317eb-d1ce-5f42-b69b-a37e643b865b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049653}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106111} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287856} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441586}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Angle and Magnitude"} :step/id #uuid "e6fd410f-37e5-5a4e-a29d-c160c7d2a397" :step/math-academy-id 121756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106564}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106564 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107167 :tutorial/content "We have been using the following formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} =\\text{comp}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a} \\cdot \\mathbf{u}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can write an alternative formula using the formula for the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the original formula for the vector projection, and also substituting $\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{| \\, \\mathbf{b} \\, |},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|}\\cdot \\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,{|}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, another general formula for the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e4098700-6d5c-5618-90a8-8dea2d4dbed0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9052 :tutorial/title "A General Formula"} :step/id #uuid "a78e2420-8af1-50c7-9a19-b4ceac9bcde4" :step/math-academy-id 121759 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106565}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106565 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105702 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105479 :question/id #uuid "58b2269d-f998-5ba5-9df8-54ea422c43aa" :question/math-academy-id "e-14618"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0670172-77cb-5e88-b78d-e5136c06095f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287859} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287861} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287937} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287938} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299633} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299635} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441587}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Projection Using Components"} :step/id #uuid "af715941-1577-597e-b254-6bc4aa300b8f" :step/math-academy-id 121760 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106567}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106566 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105703 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105480 :question/id #uuid "663f3f5e-56cf-5517-ba1e-d1a355767e0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14619"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84c96375-27d5-5ada-a952-34e09b3e92bf" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290294} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299661} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299663} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186339003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441581} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441588}] :knowledge-point/title "Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "146819e4-ecad-5e47-8884-a05b59624d07" :step/math-academy-id 121763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106567 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107168 :tutorial/content "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](images/91/9163eda36b752f1e36195b8b5a1d40bf545ec21a3988f5480d6ddcbb926c00ea.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "97117437-4034-5cb8-84a6-675d75d4f874" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9059 :tutorial/title "Projecting Perpendicular Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "ec38d18e-577a-5ebc-8349-60991ef75b80" :step/math-academy-id 121766 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106566}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107181 :tutorial/content "When finding the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b},$ we sometimes find that the resulting projection vector points in the opposite direction to $\\mathbf{b}.$ This happens when the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ extends in the direction opposite to that of $\\mathbf{b}.$\n\n![](images/0e/0e973e6f6484a9aad863ebfe89fc1ea6356d6a50844931306c6aa2b63be18a8a.png)\n\nConsider two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b},$ where it is known that \n\n$$\n|\\,\\mathbf{a}\\,|=2,\\qquad |\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|=3,\\qquad \\theta = 135^\\circ.\n$$\n\nLet's calculate $\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}}\\,\\mathbf{a}.$ \n\nFirst, notice that the angle between $\\mathbf a$ and $\\mathbf b$ is obtuse. This means we expect the projection vector to point in the opposite direction to that of $\\mathbf b.$\n\nNext, we compute the scalar projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the scalar projection is negative. \n\nThen, we find the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{|\\,\\mathbf{b}\\,|} = \\dfrac{\\mathbf{b}}{3} = \\dfrac{1}{3} \\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & ={\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a\\cdot u \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}b \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis vector points in the direction opposite to $\\mathbf{b},$ as expected." :tutorial/id #uuid "2df5fde5-316c-5962-8f0d-56362287c447" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9677 :tutorial/title "The Direction of the Projection Vector"} :step/id #uuid "fdc39c18-bf51-5a32-a828-404d1e341f7a" :step/math-academy-id 187844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106563}}] :activity/title "Calculating a Vector Projection" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105967}]}
